const enBody = "\n<p>Anthropic shipped <strong>Claude Fable 5.2</strong> and <strong>Claude Mythos 5.2</strong> on <strong>November 9, 2026</strong>, and the headline is not a capability jump \u2014 it is a <strong>20% price cut with a faster, cheaper frontier model</strong>. The new flagship pair lands at <strong>$8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output tokens</strong>, down from $10/$50 on Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1, while generating output up to 30% faster and adding an optional <strong>fast mode</strong> that runs up to 2.5x quicker for $16/$80.</p>\n\n<p>If you run long-horizon agents or heavy reasoning workloads on the Claude API, this is the cheapest the frontier has ever been \u2014 but the migration is not free. Fable 5.2 also turns on Anthropic's <strong>prefix check for every account</strong>, which turns a common \"edit the conversation history\" pattern into a 400 error. This review covers the pricing, the effort-vs-cost curves that make the cut real, the API changes, and how to migrate without breaking a production integration.</p>\n\n<h2>What launched on November 9, 2026</h2>\n<p>Fable 5.2 and Mythos 5.2 are the same underlying model. Fable carries Anthropic's standard production safeguards and is generally available to all API customers; Mythos relaxes the cyber and biology safeguards and is gated behind Anthropic's verification programs, such as the Cyber Verification Program and the Life Sciences Verification Program. The shared spec sheet:</p>\n<table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6\">\n<thead><tr class=\"bg-gray-100\"><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Model</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">API ID</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Context</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Max output</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Price / MTok</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Availability</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Claude Fable 5.2</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\"><code>claude-fable-5-2</code></td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">1M</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">128K</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$8 / $40</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">All customers</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Claude Mythos 5.2</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\"><code>claude-mythos-5-2</code></td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">1M</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">128K</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$8 / $40</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Verified orgs only</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both ship with a 1M-token context window (default and maximum), 128K max output, always-on adaptive thinking that cannot be disabled, and the newer Opus-4.7-generation tokenizer. Adaptive thinking is controlled with the <code>effort</code> parameter, and the default effort on Fable 5.2 is <code>high</code> \u2014 one notch above Opus 5.5's <code>medium</code>. The model also exposes a June 2026 knowledge cutoff.</p>\n\n<h2>Pricing: every rate, and what actually changed</h2>\n<p>The headline rate fell 20%, and every derived rate fell in step. Here is the complete Fable 5.2 price stack against Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5:</p>\n<table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6\">\n<thead><tr class=\"bg-gray-100\"><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Rate</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Fable 5.2</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Fable 5.1</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Opus 5.5</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Base input</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$8 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$10 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$4 / MTok</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Base output</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$40 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$50 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$20 / MTok</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">5-min cache write</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$10 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$12.50 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$5 / MTok</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">1-hr cache write</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$16 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$20 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$8 / MTok</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Cache hit / refresh</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$0.20 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$0.25 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$0.20 / MTok</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Fast mode input</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$16 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Fast mode output</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$80 / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Batch (50% off)</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$4 / $20</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$5 / $25</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$2 / $10</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Two things to notice. First, the cache-read rate fell from $0.25 to $0.20 per MTok, continuing the 75% cut that arrived with 5.1 \u2014 cached multi-turn agents now pay almost nothing for context. Second, fast mode doubles the token price but is a per-request toggle, not a separate model: you set <code>speed</code> on an individual Messages API request and pay the premium only on the calls that need it.</p>\n\n<h2>The effort-vs-cost curve: why the cut is bigger than 20%</h2>\n<p>The list price tells only half the story. Anthropic publishes per-effort cost curves showing what a task actually costs, and the Fable 5.2 curves are dramatically flatter than its predecessor's. On FrontierCode 2.0 \u2014 a benchmark that scores whether a model's code changes would be merged by open-source maintainers \u2014 the picture looks like this:</p>\n<table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6\">\n<thead><tr class=\"bg-gray-100\"><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Model</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Effort</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">FrontierCode score</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Cost / task</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Fable 5.2</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">High</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">45.3%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$6.72</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Opus 5.5</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">High</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">34.5%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$2.42</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">GPT-6 Astra</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">High</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">38.3%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$4.28</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Fable 5.2</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Xhigh</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">49.1%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$14.19</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Opus 5.5</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Xhigh</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">43.4%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$5.18</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">GPT-6 Astra</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Xhigh</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">41.4%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$4.54</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Read the High row first: Fable 5.2 matches Opus 5.5's best score (45.3% vs. 45.5%) for <strong>roughly half the cost per task</strong>, and clears GPT-6 Astra's best (42.8%) at about the same cost. At Xhigh, Fable 5.2 reaches 49.1% \u2014 the highest score of any released model Anthropic has tested. Anthropic's own summary: at High effort Fable 5.2 is level with Opus 5.5 at Max effort for about half the cost, and ahead of GPT-6 Astra's best at about the same cost.</p>\n<p>The same pattern repeats across the other evaluations. At Medium effort on Perplexity's WANDR agent benchmark, Fable 5.2 matches Opus 5.5's top score for about 40% of the cost per task. On Humanity's Last Exam at Low effort it matches Opus 5.5's top score for less than one-tenth of the cost. On Chartography at Medium effort it scores 66.4% \u2014 ahead of Opus 5.5 at Max (64.4%) \u2014 for under one-tenth of the cost. On BenchCAD 2.1 hardware design at Max effort it posts 83.5, versus Opus 5.5 at 65.3 and Fable 5.1 at 44.4. The 20% sticker cut, in other words, understates the real reduction on capability-matched work.</p>\n\n<h2>API and developer experience</h2>\n<p>Fable 5.2 runs on the standard Messages API with the existing SDKs. A minimal call looks identical to any other Claude model:</p>\n<pre class=\"bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm mb-6\"><code class=\"language-python\">import anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\n\nmessage = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-fable-5-2\",\n    max_tokens=4096,\n    thinking={\"type\": \"enabled\", \"budget_tokens\": 10000},\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Refactor this function to be async.\"}],\n)\nprint(message.content)</code></pre>\n<p>Fast mode is a single extra field on the request \u2014 no beta header required:</p>\n<pre class=\"bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm mb-6\"><code class=\"language-python\">response = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-fable-5-2\",\n    max_tokens=4096,\n    speed=\"fast\",          # up to 2.5x output tokens/sec\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarize this 1M-token codebase.\"}],\n)</code></pre>\n<p>A few parameter rules to internalize. Adaptive thinking is always on, and <code>effort</code> (not <code>temperature</code>) is the control for depth. Anthropic recommends omitting <code>temperature</code>, <code>top_p</code>, and <code>top_k</code>; setting them to non-default values can return a 400. Forced tool use (<code>tool_choice</code> types <code>any</code> and <code>tool</code>) also returns a 400 on this model. And note the data posture: Fable 5.2 requires 30-day data retention and is not available under zero data retention unless Anthropic expressly authorizes it.</p>\n\n<h2>Breaking change: the prefix check is now on for everyone</h2>\n<p>The single biggest migration risk is not a price or a parameter \u2014 it is Anthropic's preserved-thinking <strong>prefix check</strong>. The API tests whether anything before a Fable 5.2 thinking block has changed since the model produced it: the <code>system</code> prompt, the <code>tools</code> definition, or any earlier message. On Fable 5.2 and Mythos 5.2 this check returns a 400 error on <strong>every account, regardless of age</strong>. (On Fable 5.1 the same error only fires on accounts created on or after August 31, 2026, which means code that silently edits its history can run fine on an older account and break the moment you switch to 5.2.)</p>\n<p>The failure signature is specific. When the check fails the request returns a 400 whose message reads <code>The block is bound to a different conversation</code>. If your code edits the system prompt, mutates a tool, or rewrites an earlier turn and then sends back a 5.2 thinking block from a later turn, the call dies. Two escape hatches exist: send the <code>thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01</code> beta header and set <code>thinking.block_binding.prefix_mismatch_behavior</code> to <code>drop_block</code>, which drops the mismatched blocks and continues (surfacing them in a top-level <code>input_transformations</code> array), or stop editing the prefix entirely. Anthropic is explicit that <code>drop_block</code> is a stopgap \u2014 dropping blocks can raise output tokens.</p>\n<p>If you use Claude Code, claude.ai, Claude Managed Agents, or the Claude Agent SDK to build requests, nothing changes \u2014 those tools already send each assistant turn back exactly as returned. The risk is concentrated in integrations that hand-build message arrays and mutate history.</p>\n<p>Two adjacent changes ride along. The older <code>computer_20251124</code> tool now returns a 400 on every platform except Amazon Bedrock; use the <code>computer_toolset_20260801</code> toolset instead. And with the beta advisor tool, a Fable 5.2 or Mythos 5.2 executor accepts only another Fable 5.2 or Mythos 5.2 as its advisor \u2014 any other advisor model returns a 400.</p>\n\n<h2>Mid-conversation model switching and thinking portability</h2>\n<p>Fable 5.2 also changes how reasoning travels between models. On the Claude API and on Claude Models on Google Cloud (Google managed), moving a conversation <strong>onto</strong> Fable 5.2 or Mythos 5.2 from Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 5.5, Fable 5.1, Mythos 5.1, Opus 5, Fable 5, or earlier Opus/Sonnet/Haiku models keeps the existing reasoning \u2014 those thinking blocks remain readable. Moving <strong>away</strong> from Fable 5.2 to any other model \u2014 including Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 \u2014 drops the 5.2-produced thinking blocks, and the request still succeeds; dropped blocks are not billed. With the binding-controls beta header, the API reports each drop in <code>input_transformations</code>. Thinking blocks are also account-scoped: a block produced by one account is readable only by that account or one linked to it.</p>\n\n<h2>Benchmarks: where Fable 5.2 wins, and where it does not</h2>\n<p>The capability story is strongest on long-horizon, judgment-heavy work. Anthropic reports that over 90% of the changes merged into its own core codebase are now written by Claude with human guidance, and that an agent running an early Fable 5.2 migrated a critical production service from Python to Rust \u2014 a service that now starts about 26 times faster. In one experiment a single engineer gave Fable 5.2 an empty folder and the task of designing a prototype operating system; the model coordinated over 1,000 subagents for eight days and produced a working OS that boots on a laptop. On the cyber side, Mythos 5.2 found 10.2% more published CVEs than Opus 5.5 and 14.4% more than Mythos 5.1 on Anthropic's CVE Rediscovery run against 1,829 CVEs, and roughly doubled Mythos 5.1's vulnerability-discovery metrics in a partner's hardening campaign.</p>\n<p>Where does it not win? The honest constraint is that Fable and Mythos are positioned for the most ambitious, open-ended technical work, and Anthropic itself recommends starting most coding and knowledge tasks on Opus 5.5 and only stepping up to Fable \u2014 or down to Sonnet 5.5 or Haiku 5.5 \u2014 as the scope demands. The Max-effort tier on Fable 5.2 is also expensive: a FrontierCode Max task runs about $44.70, and several of the long-horizon benchmark Max rows run $50 or more per task. Fable 5.2 is best thought of as the high ceiling you reach for when Opus 5.5's ceiling is not enough, not as a default replacement for it.</p>\n\n<h2>Fable 5.2 vs Opus 5.5 vs GPT-6 Astra</h2>\n<table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6\">\n<thead><tr class=\"bg-gray-100\"><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Dimension</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Fable 5.2</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">Opus 5.5</th><th class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left\">GPT-6 Astra</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Price / MTok</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$8 / $40</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">$4 / $20</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">~$2 / ~$10</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">FrontierCode best</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">49.1%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">45.5%</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">42.8%</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Positioning</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Frontier, long-horizon</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Daily driver</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">General agentic</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Default effort</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">high</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">medium</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Fast mode</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">2.5x, $16/$80</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">\u2014</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Best for</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Ambitious, open-ended work</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Most coding + knowledge</td><td class=\"border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2\">Cost-sensitive agents</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For multi-provider routing, the frontier models are also available through aggregators \u2014 <a href=\"https://openrouter.ai\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:underline\">OpenRouter</a> lists <code>anthropic/claude-fable-5.2</code> at $8/$40 per 1M with a $0.20 cache read, matching Anthropic's first-party rate, and a half-price <code>:batch</code> variant. If you orchestrate across several frontier APIs from one key, a gateway such as <a href=\"https://portkey.ai/?ref=apirank\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:underline\">Portkey</a> or an aggregator such as <a href=\"https://freemodel.dev/invite/FRE-7a3b6220\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:underline\">FreeModel</a> can centralize the routing, caching, and per-request <code>speed</code> toggles across all of them.</p>\n\n<h2>Who should migrate to Fable 5.2</h2>\n<p>Start with the prefix check, not the price. If your integration appends assistant turns exactly as the API returns them and never edits the system prompt, tools, or earlier messages, the migration is a one-line model-ID change and an immediate 20% bill reduction plus faster output. If your code mutates conversation history \u2014 a memory layer that rewrites old turns, a summarizer that replaces earlier messages, or a prompt-management system that edits the system string mid-thread \u2014 audit against the prefix rules before you flip the ID, or set the binding-controls header and treat dropped blocks as a signal to refactor. Mythos 5.1 users have a second check: the prefix error already fires on every account for Mythos, so 5.2's behavior will be familiar. For most teams the recommendation is unchanged: keep Opus 5.5 as the daily driver, and reach for Fable 5.2 when a task is genuinely long-horizon or when Opus 5.5's ceiling is not enough.</p>\n\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>Claude Fable 5.2 is the clearest value upgrade in Anthropic's 2026 line-up: a 20% price cut, up to 30% faster output, and a 2.5x fast-mode toggle, on a model that beats Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra on FrontierCode for roughly half the cost per task at matched effort. The capability is real, and the cost curves are unusually favorable. The trade-off is concentrated and knowable \u2014 a strict prefix check that punishes integrations editing their own history, a required 30-day data-retention posture, and a Max-effort tier that is expensive. Migrate if your conversation plumbing is clean; if it is not, fix the plumbing first, because the prefix check is now enforced for every account.</p>\n";
